WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Mentor: Let's walk through how to actually evaluate whether retrieval is
any good, instead of just eyeballing a few example queries and calling
it done.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Mentor: You need a fixed evaluation set — a list of realistic queries,
each paired with the documents that are actually the correct answer for
that query. Without that ground truth, you have no way to measure
whether a change to your search pipeline made things better or worse.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Mentor: Then for each query, you run it through retrieval, take the
top-k results, and compute three standard metrics: precision at k,
recall at k, and mean reciprocal rank.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Mentor: Precision at k is: of the k documents you retrieved, what
fraction were actually relevant. Recall at k is: of all the documents
that were actually relevant, what fraction did you manage to retrieve in
your top k. Reciprocal rank is one over the position of the first
relevant result — so if the correct document is ranked first, that's a
perfect score of 1, and it drops off fast the further down it appears.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Intern: What if the eval set references a document that hasn't actually
been ingested yet — does that just tank the score?

00:01:35.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Mentor: It shouldn't. If you silently score an unresolvable query as
zero, that number gets mixed in with real retrieval failures, and you
can't tell the difference between "the eval fixture has a typo" and
"retrieval is actually broken." Better to skip it and report the skip
count separately, so a bad fixture entry doesn't masquerade as a
retrieval regression.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Mentor: One more practical note — don't hardcode document IDs into the
eval set. IDs are assigned at ingestion time and change every time the
corpus gets reseeded. Reference documents by title instead, and resolve
titles to whatever the current IDs are at evaluation time.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Intern: That makes sense — otherwise the eval set silently rots the
first time someone re-ingests everything.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.000
Mentor: Exactly right.
